  "subject": "CA-017 / #45 CLIENT HALF COMPLETE and the conformance-harness gap CLOSED, all in the working tree and UNCOMMITTED. Suite 165 → 174 green. Headline criterion verified: ft-renderer-catio-08 loads canonical/riverside-heritage-reference-v1.4.opencatalog (24 members, all deflated), resolves 12 items and 23 photos, zero unresolved refs — the reference renderer now reads the reference catalog. DecompressionStream('deflate-raw') VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY, not taken on your word: MDN browser-compat-data gives Chrome 103 / Firefox 113 / Safari 16.4, plus a live byte-identical round-trip in Node v24.14.1 against Python zlib wbits=-15. The assumption HOLDS — Firefox and Safari gained deflate-raw in the same release as the constructor, so the only exposure is Chrome 80-102 where the constructor exists but the format throws TypeError; handled by catching it into a diagnostic that names the version floor, and by constructing the stream LAZILY so a stored-only bundle (the adopter's shape) opens even without deflate-raw support. Took your simplification, and it pays twice: making the client read resp.arrayBuffer() rather than resp.json() means the client half works against the proxy AS IT BEHAVES TODAY and keeps working unchanged after the proxy stops decoding, so the two halves are no longer coupled by a flag day. Closed the harness gap by EXTRACTION rather than a second file: splitting the loader into renderer/catio.js would silently break renderer/README self-host path 1 ('download index.html and open it') — an L1 regression a classic script src does not avoid either — so the loader stays inside index.html behind CATIO-LOADER-BEGIN/END markers that the test slices out and runs under Node, with all assertions in Python and the driver carrying NO import logic of its own. RAN NEGATIVE CONTROLS because a test that cannot fail is worthless: swapping the central-directory size for the local-header field turns the data-descriptor test red (Z_BUF_ERROR), and swapping deflate-raw for deflate turns 6 of 9 red; that second control also exposed a weakness in my own assertion, which passed on a substring that also appears in an error message, now tightened to match the constructor call. FIXTURE DECISION on the merits: generate at test time, do NOT commit the 267 KB binary — it matches the test_catio_bundle.py:22 convention, and real JPEG bytes hash no more convincingly than deterministic blobs when the test asserts SHA-256 against what it packed; real photos matter for VISUAL verification, which is a browser task. GOVERNANCE FINDING NEEDING YOUR CALL: the deploy bound is narrower than the deploy surface. CA-016 made the renderer a RUNTIME FETCH (catdef.org/src/index.ts:378 pins RENDERER_URL to raw.githubusercontent.com/catdef/catdef-spec/main/renderer/index.html, 5-minute edge TTL), so a push of renderer/index.html to CATDEF-SPEC main deploys to render.catdef.org within five minutes with no catdef.org deploy at all and no staging step — the exact property that put the gate on the other repo. Both briefs state the bound as catdef.org only. I have therefore COMMITTED NOTHING. NOT VERIFIED: the browser half — the Claude-in-Chrome extension is not connected in this session, so DOM glue, blob URLs, img display, the gallery and the card hero are code-complete but unverified; I am NOT claiming 'photos displayed'. SCOPE LINE DRAWN: rotation, caption, slot ordering and is_primary implemented; crop_mode and deskew NOT implemented, because correct freeform crop needs real geometry, the canonical bundle does use crop_mode, and rendering the reference catalog WRONGLY is worse than rendering it uncropped — flagged rather than half-done, wants its own item. Proxy half not started, per your sequencing.",

