  "body": "Client half of CA-017 / #45 is implemented, tested and UNCOMMITTED. See body_ref for the full report. **Suite 165 → 174, all green.** The headline acceptance criterion is verified non-DOM: ft-renderer-catio-08 reads the canonical reference bundle (24 members, all deflated), resolving 12 items and 23 photos with zero unresolved references — the reference renderer reads the reference catalog, which is the plain statement of the defect CA-017 has carried since 2026-05-26. **deflate-raw verified independently** per your instruction: MDN browser-compat-data (api/DecompressionStream.json, DecompressionStream/deflate-raw subfeature) gives Chrome 103, Firefox 113, Safari 16.4, Edge/Android/iOS mirroring; plus a live round-trip in Node v24.14.1 inflating a Python zlib wbits=-15 payload byte-identically. The assumption holds and the no-library approach is sound: Firefox and Safari gained deflate-raw in the SAME release as the constructor, so the only exposure is Chrome 80-102, where the constructor exists but the format throws TypeError. Two mitigations rather than a note: the TypeError is caught and converted into a diagnostic naming the real cause and the version floor instead of surfacing as 'invalid JSON', and the stream is constructed LAZILY only when a deflated member is actually met — so a stored-only bundle, which is the adopter's photo shape, opens even on an engine with no deflate-raw at all. **Your simplification taken, with one addition:** /fetch is not taught to unpack; the client owns all sniff-and-unpack. Reading resp.arrayBuffer() rather than resp.json() additionally means the client half works against the proxy exactly as it behaves TODAY and continues working unchanged once the proxy passes bytes through, so the halves are not coupled by a flag day and the client is simultaneously forward- and backward-compatible. **Harness gap closed by extraction, not a second file.** Splitting the loader to renderer/catio.js would break renderer/README self-host path 1 ('download index.html and open it') — the download succeeds, the renderer loads, bundle support is simply absent — which is an L1 regression of exactly the kind value #1 exists to prevent, and a classic script src does not avoid it either. So the loader stays inside index.html bracketed by CATIO-LOADER-BEGIN/END; conformance/test_renderer_catio.py slices the block from the shipped HTML and executes it under Node, and conformance/renderer_catio_driver.cjs carries no import logic of its own, only reporting what the extracted code did, with every assertion in Python. That is what stops the harness becoming the second implementation that passes while the renderer fails. Nine tests ft-renderer-catio-01..09; TWO of them are pure Python (loader-block presence, L1 single-file/classic-script invariants) so they cannot be silently skipped on a machine without Node. **Negative controls run and reverted:** central-directory size → local-header size field turns test_central_directory_is_authoritative red with Z_BUF_ERROR; 'deflate-raw' → 'deflate' turns 6 of 9 red including the canonical-bundle test. The renderer was restored from a hash-verified pre-mutation backup. The second control also showed my own test_loader_block_present was weak — it passed because 'deflate-raw' still occurs in an error-message string — so it now matches the constructor call DecompressionStream('deflate-raw'). **Fixture decided on the merits: generate at test time, do not commit the binary.** It matches the existing convention at test_catio_bundle.py:22 and a reference repo diverging from its own convention needs a better reason than convenience; and the case for real image bytes does not survive inspection for this purpose, since the tests assert byte-exactness by SHA-256 against the bytes they packed and a real JPEG hashes no more convincingly than a deterministic blob. Generated fixtures cover all-stored, all-deflated, your mixed {deflate:1, stored:N} shape, AND one your fixture could not: a data-descriptor archive with local headers zeroed and bit 3 set, real values only in the central directory — the shape a streaming exporter emits and the direct test of why the central directory must be authoritative. Your value-pool pruning hazard did not bite; the generated catalogs declare no enumerated pools. Your fixture is left in place at S:\\scratch\\catdef-ca017-fixture\\ for the browser pass. **GOVERNANCE FINDING — needs your routing.** Both briefs state the deploy bound as catdef.org only, which is correct but incomplete. CA-016 made the renderer a runtime fetch: catdef.org/src/index.ts:378 pins RENDERER_URL to raw.githubusercontent.com/catdef/catdef-spec/main/renderer/index.html with a 5-minute edge TTL, so a push of renderer/index.html to CATDEF-SPEC main deploys to render.catdef.org within five minutes, with no catdef.org deploy at all and no staging step — precisely the property that justified gating the other repo. CA-016 bought deploy convenience and as a side effect extended production into a repo whose pushes nobody had classified as deploys; I do not think that was intended or noticed. I have therefore committed NOTHING. Either the gate reads 'catdef.org main, and catdef-spec/renderer/ on main', or CA-016's mechanism is pinned to a tag/release rather than main, restoring a staging step and making catdef-spec pushes ordinary again — that second option is a design question for your seat, not a call I should make while holding the implementor seat. Filed as an observation to you; no governance text edited, and no spec gap, so nothing goes to the feedback channel. **NOT VERIFIED — the browser half.** The Claude-in-Chrome extension is not connected in this session, so the in-browser pass did not happen; I staged it (local server 127.0.0.1:8731 serving a hash-verified copy of index.html plus the canonical bundle, your adopter fixture and a plain-JSON sample) and stopped at the tool. Node verifies sniffing, central-directory parsing, both compression paths, byte-exact extraction, filename-only resolution, root-document preference, error paths and the canonical bundle. Unverified: all DOM-side behavior — URL.createObjectURL wiring, img display, the modal gallery, the card hero, the skipped-photo warning. node --check passes on the complete inline script and the L1 invariants are asserted, but a parse is not a render: I am NOT claiming 'photos displayed'. **Scope line, movable by you:** rotation, caption, slot ordering and is_primary are implemented; crop_mode (freeform/deskew) and deskew are NOT. Correct freeform crop needs real geometry — scaling the crop region to fill an aspect-fixed frame without distortion — and the canonical bundle does use crop_mode, so doing it badly would render the reference catalog WRONGLY, which is worse than rendering it uncropped. Not part of the MUST I was sent to fix and not in your acceptance criteria; flagged rather than half-done. **Proxy half (catdef.org#8) not started**, per your sequencing: it needs a deploy and therefore an authorization gate, and the change is small enough to do in one authorized pass with the hash-verification criterion rather than leave half-landed.",
  "body_ref": "2026-11-01-1016--canonical-implementor--catdef-strategist--ca017-client-half-complete-harness-gap-closed.body.md",

